er/id #uuid "d0b85a9a-afc4-5057-b50b-a3fabfd591bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b57f374-7974-5938-b11d-cfd731887fe8" :answer/id #uuid "2b57f374-7974-5938-b11d-cfd731887fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬X∨¬Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42a65b2d-e5f8-5a76-b55d-46a8613eea7f" :answer/id #uuid "42a65b2d-e5f8-5a76-b55d-46a8613eea7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬Y∧X" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33e6f69b-ceb9-546e-bc81-3a178a362d67" :answer/id #uuid "33e6f69b-ceb9-546e-bc81-3a178a362d67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬Y∨X" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77